1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,235
I want my own smart speaker:

2
00:00:02,235 --> 00:00:04,530
a voice assistant that runs at home.

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:06,506
The electronics are on my desk.

4
00:00:06,506 --> 00:00:08,100
What's missing is a case.

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:17,117
Today I'll show you how an AI agent designs that case around the real board and speaker, with mic holes, a light ring and a grille, ready to print.

6
00:00:17,117 --> 00:00:18,475
No CAD knowledge needed.

7
00:00:21,751 --> 00:00:22,473
We use SOMA.

8
00:00:22,473 --> 00:00:26,441
It's free and open source, and you can download it at somacad.app.

9
00:00:28,091 --> 00:00:32,862
The heart of it is the reSpeaker XVF3800 from Seeed Studio:

10
00:00:32,862 --> 00:00:40,301
four microphones, a ring of twelve LEDs, two buttons, and a small XIAO ESP32-S3 on the back.

11
00:00:41,258 --> 00:00:45,365
Seeed publishes a 3D file of the board on its wiki, under Creative Commons.

12
00:00:45,365 --> 00:00:49,308
They even have a flat case for it, but I want one with a speaker inside.

13
00:00:50,058 --> 00:00:51,628
So we download the 3D file.

14
00:00:53,278 --> 00:00:56,856
In SOMA, we start a new model and import two files:

15
00:00:56,856 --> 00:01:02,048
the board's STEP file, and the datasheet of my speaker, a Visaton FRS 5 X.

16
00:01:03,211 --> 00:01:06,005
Click the STEP file, and SOMA shows the board:

17
00:01:06,005 --> 00:01:09,650
the LED ring on top, the XIAO and the connectors underneath.

18
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:13,319
I trimmed Seeed's file a little beforehand:

19
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:20,707
the board, connectors, buttons, LEDs and microphones stay as they are, the tiny chips became simple blocks.

20
00:01:20,707 --> 00:01:22,640
That keeps every build fast.

21
00:01:23,411 --> 00:01:25,265
For the speaker there's no 3D file.

22
00:01:25,265 --> 00:01:27,701
But the datasheet has every dimension we need.

23
00:01:29,545 --> 00:01:34,065
On the right, we start Claude Code, our agent, right inside the model folder.

24
00:01:36,011 --> 00:01:40,246
First, I ask it to bring in the board, and to build the speaker from its datasheet.

25
00:01:41,713 --> 00:01:48,083
The agent reads the STEP file and the PDF, finds the board's mounting holes and the speaker's size, and places both parts.

26
00:01:49,035 --> 00:01:49,812
Here they are.

27
00:01:49,812 --> 00:01:53,533
The structure tree lists the board with its parts, and the speaker.

28
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:54,644
Both are references:

29
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:56,365
see-through, and never printed.

30
00:01:58,102 --> 00:01:58,877
Now the case.

31
00:01:58,877 --> 00:02:01,260
I describe it the way I'd tell a friend:

32
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:10,792
holes above the microphones, a light ring above the LEDs, a grille for the speaker, buttons and ports reachable, screws that match the board, and easy to print.

33
00:02:11,492 --> 00:02:13,528
The agent works through it step by step.

34
00:02:13,528 --> 00:02:19,942
It writes the model as code, SOMA builds it after every change, and the agent looks at pictures of its own result to check it.

35
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:22,345
And here's my smart speaker.

36
00:02:23,045 --> 00:02:25,336
The structure tree shows what it made:

37
00:02:25,336 --> 00:02:30,399
the housing, the light ring and the base, plus standard screws and heat-set inserts.

38
00:02:30,399 --> 00:02:33,895
The board and the speaker are still inside, as references.

39
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:37,334
Does it all fit?

40
00:02:37,334 --> 00:02:39,232
The section tool cuts the case open.

41
00:02:39,232 --> 00:02:43,820
The board hangs from posts under the lid, and the speaker sits right behind its grille.

42
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:45,592
A nice detail:

43
00:02:45,592 --> 00:02:50,922
the agent noticed that the board has only three mounting holes, not four, and used all three.

44
00:02:50,922 --> 00:02:54,360
The side buttons press through flexible tongues in the wall.

45
00:02:55,257 --> 00:02:57,744
The explode view shows how it goes together:

46
00:02:57,744 --> 00:03:04,187
the light ring presses in from the top, and the base with the speaker slides in from below, closed by four screws.

47
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:07,958
I'd like to hang it on the wall.

48
00:03:07,958 --> 00:03:12,220
So I turn it around, click Mark up, and draw where the keyhole should go.

49
00:03:13,023 --> 00:03:15,153
Send it to the agent, with one sentence.

50
00:03:19,738 --> 00:03:22,028
And there's the keyhole on the back, right where I drew it.

51
00:03:22,778 --> 00:03:25,619
The agent also turned the main sizes into parameters.

52
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:27,548
You find them in the Parameters tab.

53
00:03:28,248 --> 00:03:30,678
A thicker wall, bigger grille holes:

54
00:03:30,678 --> 00:03:33,243
drag a slider, and the model rebuilds.

55
00:03:33,243 --> 00:03:34,458
No agent, no code.

56
00:03:35,158 --> 00:03:37,608
Reset all brings back the values from the code.

57
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:40,494
Finally, Export.

58
00:03:40,494 --> 00:03:41,808
All parts as 3MF:

59
00:03:41,808 --> 00:03:45,517
one file per printed part, ready for the slicer.

60
00:03:45,517 --> 00:03:49,148
The board, the speaker and the screws stay out.

61
00:03:50,292 --> 00:03:55,200
From a board on my desk to a case that's ready to print, without drawing a single line.

62
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,062
Get SOMA for free at somacad.app.
